channel                é                        what the scheme wraps
JSON string            "\u00e9"                 UTF-16 code units
URL percent-encoding   %C3%A9                   BYTES -- of an unnamed encoding
MIME encoded-word      =?utf-8?q?=C3=A9?=       bytes, of a charset it NAMES
punycode (IDNA)        xn--caf-dma              code points -- no bytes at all

THE SAME FOUR ON AN ASTRAL CHARACTER, WHERE THEY SEPARATE
   JSON     "\ud83d\ude00"   TWO escapes -- a surrogate pair, because
                            \u is a 16-bit code unit and nothing bigger
   URL      %F0%9F%98%80   four bytes, four %XX, because UTF-8
                            spells it in four -- and nothing in the URL
                            says that it was UTF-8

WHY THE UNIT MATTERS MORE THAN THE SPELLING

JSON escapes CODE UNITS, so its escape hatch inherits UTF-16's shape --
which is how a JSON document, whose transport is UTF-8 by RFC 8259, ends
up containing a surrogate pair that has no UTF-8 encoding at all.

%XX escapes BYTES and never records whose. urllib defaults to UTF-8,
but Latin-1 would give %E9 for the same character, and the URL is
the same shape either way. The receiver has to be told out of band --
which is why old query strings are a graveyard of guessed charsets.

A MIME encoded-word is the only one of the four that CARRIES the charset
name inside the encoded text. That is why an email header can mix
charsets word by word, and why it is the least ambiguous of the four.

Punycode does not touch bytes. It re-spells the sequence of CODE POINTS
as ASCII letters using a delta-encoding, so it has no encoding
parameter to get wrong -- and it is why an internationalized domain has
an xn-- prefix rather than a percent sign.

So 'it is escaped' is not a description. Ask WHICH UNIT is escaped, and
you have also asked which layer is allowed to decode it.
